    "zig-structs-learn": {
      "id": "zig-structs-learn",
      "title": "Learn: a shape, and the functions that work on it",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "zig-structs"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Name the fields of a struct and say what each one means to the sheet.",
        "Explain why a method takes the shape as its first argument and what that argument is called.",
        "Describe what a tagged union holds and how the tag says which field is there now.",
        "Build a cell from its text and read the amount back out of it"
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "A struct is a shape: the fields it has, and what each of them means. The sheet's cell is a struct, and the fields are the text it was read from and the amount it holds."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The cell, out of src/cells.zig:",
          "code": "pub const Cell = struct {\n    /// The text the cell was read from.\n    text: []const u8,\n    /// The number the cell holds.\n    amount: Amount,"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "A function that works on a cell can live inside the struct, and then it is called a method. A method is a function whose first argument is the shape it belongs to, and that argument is named self. The sheet's cell has one: of, which builds a cell from its text."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "A method, and the call it answers to, out of src/cells.zig:",
          "code": "    pub fn of(text: []const u8) Cell {\n        _ = text;\n        // TODO: a cell keeps the text it was read from and the amount in it.\n        return .{ .text = \"\", .amount = 0 };\n    }"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "The braces with a dot in front are how a struct is built: .{ .text = ..., .amount = ... } names the fields as it goes, so a field that is added later is a compile error rather than a zero nobody noticed."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "The other shape in this file is a union. A union holds one of several shapes at a time, and a tagged union, written union(enum), carries a tag that says which one is there now. A cell's value is either a number, or words, or nothing at all, and the tag is what tells the reader which."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The tagged union, out of src/cells.zig:",
          "code": "pub const Value = union(enum) {\n    number: Amount,\n    text: []const u8,\n    empty: void,\n};"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "A switch over a tagged union has to mention every field, the same way a switch over an enum has to mention every case. That is what stops the sheet from reading a cell as a number when it holds words: the code that reads the tag has to say what it does with each shape, and the compiler refuses the code that does not."
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "A struct is a shape: named fields, each with a type.",
            "A method is a function whose first argument is the shape it belongs to.",
            "A tagged union holds one of several shapes and carries a tag that says which.",
            "A switch over a tagged union has to mention every field."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The cell and its tagged union, completed:",
          "code": "pub const Cell = struct {\n    text: []const u8,\n    amount: Amount,\n\n    pub fn of(text: []const u8) Cell {\n        return .{ .text = text, .amount = functions.parseAmountOrZero(text) };\n    }\n\n    pub fn value(self: Cell) Amount {\n        return self.amount;\n    }\n};\n\npub fn valueOf(text: []const u8) Value {\n    if (control_flow.isBlank(text)) return .{ .empty = {} };\n    if (control_flow.isNumber(text)) return .{ .number = functions.parseAmount(text) };\n    return .{ .text = text };\n}\n\npub fn amountOf(value: Value) Amount {\n    return switch (value) {\n        .number => |amount| amount,\n        .text, .empty => 0,\n    };\n}"
        },
        {
          "kind": "quickcheck",
          "body": "What does valueOf(\"notes\") put in the union?",
          "items": [
            ".text, holding the words \"notes\".",
            ".number, holding the amount 0.",
            ".empty, because words hold no value.",
            ".number, because every cell has a tag."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explain": "isBlank is false and isNumber is false, so the fallthrough keeps the words and tags the value as text."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The commands you run, and the one the Run button calls for you:",
          "code": "zig build test   # run the whole project's tests\nnode --test --test-reporter=tap test/cells.test.mjs   # what the Run button calls"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "Open src/cells.zig and make Cell.of build a cell that keeps the text it was read from and the amount in it, and make Cell.value give that amount back. Then run zig build test and read what the Zig test runner said."
        }
      ],
      "visualization": {
        "type": "pattern-match",
        "spec": {
          "value": ".{ .text = \"notes\" }",
          "cases": [
            {
              "pattern": ".number => |n|",
              "binds": "not this one",
              "note": "the tag is text, so the number field is not there"
            },
            {
              "pattern": ".text => |t|",
              "binds": "t = \"notes\"",
              "note": "the tag selects this branch, and t is the words"
            },
            {
              "pattern": ".empty",
              "binds": "not this one",
              "note": "the cell holds words, not nothing"
            }
          ],
          "fired": 1
        },
        "caption": "A tagged union holds one of several shapes and carries the tag that says which one is there now. The tag, not the field that happens to be first, chooses the branch."
      },
      "hints": [
        "Write down what Cell.of(\"199\").text and Cell.of(\"199\").amount have to be before you open the file.",
        "The struct is built with the field names: .{ .text = ..., .amount = ... }. The parser from the previous phase is what turns the text into an amount.",
        "A method whose first argument is the shape it belongs to can read the fields of that shape without being told which one it is.",
        "Run zig build test and read the failing test name. It names the behaviour the method has to have."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Building a struct with positional braces. Naming the fields is what makes a field that is added later a compile error rather than a silent zero.",
        "Thinking a method is a different kind of function. It is the same thing with the shape as its first argument, and that is why it can be called with a dot.",
        "Reading a tagged union as an enum with extra data. The tag is a value of its own, and the switch over it is what says which field is there."
      ],
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "Zig language reference: structs",
          "url": "https://ziglang.org/documentation/0.17.0/#Structs"
        },
        {
          "label": "Zig language reference: unions",
          "url": "https://ziglang.org/documentation/0.17.0/#Unions"
        }
      ]
    },
