    "zig-pointers-learn": {
      "id": "zig-pointers-learn",
      "title": "Learn: the address of a value",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "zig-pointers"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Explain what a pointer is and how it differs from the value it points at.",
        "Read a function signature and say whether the function can change what the caller holds.",
        "Predict what happens to a caller's amount when a function takes the amount by value.",
        "Write a function that changes an amount through its address"
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "Zig passes values by value. A function that takes an Amount gets its own copy, and when it changes that copy the caller never hears about it. When a function has to change what the caller holds, it is given the address of the value instead."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The function that changes an amount, out of src/pointers.zig:",
          "code": "pub fn setAmount(cell: *Amount, amount: Amount) void {"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "The type *Amount is a pointer to an amount: the address, not the amount. The caller takes the address of its own value with the ampersand, so the pointer names a place in the caller's memory rather than a copy of what is there."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The call that hands over the address, out of src/pointers.zig:",
          "code": "    var amount: Amount = 1;\n    setAmount(&amount, 42);"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "Inside the function, the pointer is read or written with a star: cell.* is the amount the pointer names. Writing to it changes the caller's value, and the caller sees the change when the function returns. That is the whole difference between the two signatures, and it is visible in the type before the body is read."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "Reading and writing through the pointer, out of src/pointers.zig:",
          "code": "pub fn doubleInPlace(cell: *Amount) void {\n    _ = cell;\n    // TODO: read the amount through the pointer, double it, and write it back."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "There is no null pointer to walk into by accident. A pointer always points at a value of the type it names, so a function that takes one can read it without checking anything. The cost of that safety is at the call: the caller has to have something to point at, and it has to stay alive for as long as the pointer does."
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "Zig passes values by value, so a function that takes an amount works on a copy.",
            "A pointer type is written with a star, and the caller takes the address with an ampersand.",
            "A star reads or writes through the pointer: cell.* is the value the pointer names.",
            "There is no null pointer to check for, so a pointer always names a value of its type."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The three functions, completed, each writing through the pointer:",
          "code": "pub fn setAmount(cell: *Amount, amount: Amount) void {\n    cell.* = amount;\n}\n\npub fn doubleInPlace(cell: *Amount) void {\n    cell.* = cell.* * 2;\n}\n\npub fn keepTheLarger(cell: *Amount, other: Amount) void {\n    if (other > cell.*) cell.* = other;\n}"
        },
        {
          "kind": "quickcheck",
          "body": "After setAmount(&amount, 42), what does the caller hold?",
          "items": [
            "42, because the write went through the address.",
            "1, because the function received a copy.",
            "0, because the old value is replaced first.",
            "A compile error, because the value is immutable."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explain": "The parameter is *Amount, so cell.* names the caller amount itself, and the assignment changes what the caller holds."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The commands you run, and the one the Run button calls for you:",
          "code": "zig build test   # run the whole project's tests\nnode --test --test-reporter=tap test/pointers.test.mjs   # what the Run button calls"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "Open src/pointers.zig and make setAmount write the new amount through the pointer it was given. Then run zig build test and read what the Zig test runner said."
        }
      ],
      "visualization": {
        "type": "state-compare",
        "spec": {
          "before": {
            "title": "var amount: Amount = 1",
            "lines": [
              "setAmount(&amount, 42)"
            ]
          },
          "after": {
            "title": "after the call",
            "lines": [
              "cell.* = 42",
              "amount is 42"
            ]
          }
        },
        "caption": "The caller passes the address with &amount, and the function writes 42 through it. Without the star in the signature the function would change a copy, and the caller would still hold 1."
      },
      "hints": [
        "Write down what the caller's amount has to be after setAmount(&amount, 42) before you open the file.",
        "The parameter is a pointer, so the amount is not in the function: it is at the address the pointer names.",
        "Reading or writing through a pointer is done with a star: the value the pointer names is cell.*.",
        "Run zig build test and read the failing test name. It says what the caller has to see afterwards."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Writing to the pointer itself instead of through it. The pointer is the address, and the value is what lives at the address.",
        "Passing the amount itself where the signature asks for a pointer. The compiler refuses it, and that refusal is what stops a function that means to change a value from quietly working on a copy.",
        "Thinking a pointer can be null. There is no null pointer in Zig, so a function that takes one can read it without a check."
      ],
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "Zig language reference: pointers",
          "url": "https://ziglang.org/documentation/0.17.0/#Pointers"
        },
        {
          "label": "Zig language reference: slices",
          "url": "https://ziglang.org/documentation/0.17.0/#Slices"
        }
      ],
      "bridge": {
        "course": "go-fundamentals",
        "phase": "go-pointers",
        "text": "In Go a function that takes a value works on a copy, and to change what the caller holds you take the address with &. In Zig the same rule holds, and the address is written into the type as *Amount."
      }
    },
