    "zig-errors-learn": {
      "id": "zig-errors-learn",
      "title": "Learn: a value, or the reason there is no value",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "zig-errors"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Name the failures a cell can have and say what each of them means to the reader.",
        "Explain what an error union in a return type promises the caller, and why the compiler holds the caller to it.",
        "Predict what a caller sees when a function returns an error it does not handle.",
        "Read an expression and decide which of the named failures it can have"
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "Reading a cell can fail. The text may not be an expression at all, the operator may be one the sheet does not know, and the right-hand number may be zero. A function that can fail says so in its return type, and the compiler makes every caller deal with it."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The failures the sheet names, out of src/evaluate.zig:",
          "code": "pub const ParseError = error{\n    MalformedExpression,\n    UnknownOperator,\n    DivisionByZero,\n};"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "That is an error set: a closed list of named failures. It is written like an enum, and it is a set of values the same way an enum is, so the compiler knows exactly which failures a function can have."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The return type that says a function can fail, out of src/evaluate.zig:",
          "code": "pub fn evaluate(text: []const u8) ParseError!Amount {"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "The bang is an error union: the value or one of the errors in the set before it. A caller that ignores it cannot: the compiler refuses a call whose error is dropped, so a failure cannot be forgotten three functions down. A caller either propagates it with try, or handles it with catch, or names the error in its own return type."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The two ways a caller deals with it, out of src/evaluate.zig:",
          "code": "    try std.testing.expectEqual(@as(Amount, 42), try evaluate(\"=12 + 30\"));\n    try std.testing.expectError(error.MalformedExpression, evaluate(\"12 + 30\"));"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "The error set is the point. A function that can only fail in one way says so, and a caller that only cares about that way can catch it. A function that returned a generic error would tell the caller nothing, and the caller would have to guess which failure it was."
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "An error set is a closed list of named failures, written like an enum.",
            "A bang in a return type says the function gives a value or one of those failures.",
            "The compiler refuses a call whose error is dropped, so a failure cannot be forgotten.",
            "try propagates a failure to the caller, and catch handles it where it happens."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "evaluate, completed, with every failure named in its return type:",
          "code": "pub fn evaluate(text: []const u8) ParseError!Amount {\n    if (text.len == 0 or text[0] != '=') return error.MalformedExpression;\n    const body = std.mem.trim(u8, text[1..], \" \");\n    const at = std.mem.indexOfAny(u8, body, \"+-*/\") orelse return error.MalformedExpression;\n    const left = std.mem.trim(u8, body[0..at], \" \");\n    const right = std.mem.trim(u8, body[at + 1 ..], \" \");\n    if (!control_flow.isNumber(left) or !control_flow.isNumber(right)) return error.MalformedExpression;\n    const op = optionals.opFor(body[at]) orelse return error.UnknownOperator;\n    const a = functions.parseAmount(left);\n    const b = functions.parseAmount(right);\n    if (op == .divide and b == 0) return error.DivisionByZero;\n    return ops.apply(op, a, b);\n}"
        },
        {
          "kind": "quickcheck",
          "body": "What does evaluate(\"=12 + 30\") return?",
          "items": [
            "42, as an Amount inside the error union.",
            "The text \"12 + 30\", unchanged.",
            "An error, because a plus sign is unknown.",
            "42, after printing the expression."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explain": "The text starts with =, both sides are numbers, and + is a known operator, so the function returns the sum 42."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The commands you run, and the one the Run button calls for you:",
          "code": "zig build test   # run the whole project's tests\nnode --test --test-reporter=tap test/evaluate.test.mjs   # what the Run button calls"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "Open src/evaluate.zig and make evaluate read the number, the operator and the number of an expression, and do the arithmetic. A text that is not an expression has to come back as error.MalformedExpression. Then run zig build test and read what the Zig test runner said."
        }
      ],
      "visualization": {
        "type": "error-union",
        "spec": {
          "call": "evaluate(text)",
          "type": "ParseError!Amount",
          "value": "the amount 42",
          "errors": [
            "error.MalformedExpression",
            "error.UnknownOperator",
            "error.DivisionByZero"
          ],
          "caught": "error.MalformedExpression"
        },
        "caption": "The compiler refuses a call whose error is dropped, so a failure cannot be forgotten three functions down."
      },
      "hints": [
        "Write down the three failures in the error set before you open the file, and which one of them each can happen for.",
        "An expression is a number, an operator and a number. The parser from the functions phase reads the numbers, and the operator is one byte.",
        "The compiler will not let you divide by zero, so the division has to be checked before it is done, and the error set says which error that is.",
        "Run zig build test and read the failing test name. The names are the sentences the function has to satisfy."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Returning a generic error and losing the name of the failure. The error set is what tells the caller what went wrong, and a caller that cannot tell cannot decide.",
        "Thinking try runs the next line anyway. try hands the failure to the caller and leaves, and the code after it does not run.",
        "Catching every error in one place. A catch that swallows the reason turns a failure into a zero, and the reader of the sheet never learns there was a problem."
      ],
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "Zig language reference: error unions",
          "url": "https://ziglang.org/documentation/0.17.0/#Error-Union-Type"
        },
        {
          "label": "Zig language reference: error sets",
          "url": "https://ziglang.org/documentation/0.17.0/#Error-Sets"
        }
      ],
      "bridge": {
        "course": "go-fundamentals",
        "phase": "go-errors",
        "text": "In Go a function that cannot do its job returns an error as a second value, and the failure is a value you compare with errors.Is. In Zig the two are one value, an error union, and the type names every failure it can have, so the caller is told which one it was."
      }
    },
