    "zig-defer-learn": {
      "id": "zig-defer-learn",
      "title": "Learn: cleaning up on the way out",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "direction": "guided",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "zig-defer"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Explain what defer runs and when it runs, and say what it stops you having to remember.",
        "Predict what a function does with a copy it made when the function returns early with an error.",
        "Describe what errdefer is for, and how it differs from a defer that runs on every path.",
        "Build a copy of a text, report what it measures, and give the copy back"
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "The previous phase made a function allocate. Allocating is half of the job: the other half is giving the memory back, and the half nobody gives back is the half that leaks."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "defer is how Zig ties the two together. A defer runs when the scope it was written in ends, however it ends: on the normal path, on an early return, and on a failure that leaves the scope with an error."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The copy, and the defer that gives it back, out of src/defer.zig:",
          "code": "pub fn trimmedLength(text: []const u8, allocator: std.mem.Allocator) !usize {\n    _ = text;\n    _ = allocator;\n    // TODO: copy the text, free it on the way out, and report the trimmed length.\n    return 0;\n}"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "The order matters. The defer is written right after the allocation, on the line below it, so the two are read together: this is what was taken, and this is what gives it back. A defer written further down, or in a different function, is a defer somebody has to remember to move every time the code above it changes."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "errdefer is the other one. It runs only when the scope ends because of an error, which makes it the right tool for undoing half-finished work: if the second copy cannot be made, the first one has to be given back, and the error is what says it was not finished."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The two copies, and the undoing of the first one, out of src/defer.zig:",
          "code": "pub fn duplicateTwice(text: []const u8, allocator: std.mem.Allocator) !Pair {\n    _ = text;\n    _ = allocator;\n    // TODO: make two copies, and give the first one back if the second fails.\n    return error.OutOfMemory;\n}"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "The difference between the two is the whole of the difference between a leak and a clean failure. A defer runs on every path out of the scope, so the memory is given back whether the function succeeded or not. An errdefer runs only on the error path, so it undoes only the work that did not finish."
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "defer runs when the scope ends, however it ends: normally, on an early return, and on a failure.",
            "The defer belongs on the line below the allocation it gives back.",
            "errdefer runs only when the scope ends with an error, and undoes the work that did not finish.",
            "A defer that never runs is a leak, and the testing allocator reports it."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "Both functions, completed: the defer frees the copy, and the errdefer frees the first copy when the second allocation fails.",
          "code": "pub fn trimmedLength(text: []const u8, allocator: std.mem.Allocator) !usize {\n    const copy = try allocator.dupe(u8, text);\n    defer allocator.free(copy);\n    return std.mem.trim(u8, copy, \" \").len;\n}\n\npub fn duplicateTwice(text: []const u8, allocator: std.mem.Allocator) !Pair {\n    const first = try allocator.dupe(u8, text);\n    errdefer allocator.free(first);\n    const second = try allocator.dupe(u8, text);\n    return .{ .first = first, .second = second };\n}"
        },
        {
          "kind": "quickcheck",
          "body": "When does the errdefer in duplicateTwice run?",
          "items": [
            "When the second allocation fails.",
            "After every return, successful or not.",
            "When the caller frees the pair.",
            "Before the first copy is allocated."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explain": "errdefer runs only when the scope leaves with an error, so it frees the first copy once the second allocation fails."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The commands you run, and the one the Run button calls for you:",
          "code": "zig build test   # run the whole project's tests\nnode --test --test-reporter=tap test/defer.test.mjs   # what the Run button calls"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "Open src/defer.zig and make trimmedLength copy the text, free the copy on the way out, and report the length of the copy once its spaces are gone. Then run zig build test and read what the Zig test runner said."
        }
      ],
      "visualization": {
        "type": "cleanup-paths",
        "spec": {
          "normal": [
            "alloc(copy)",
            "defer free",
            "read len",
            "return 2",
            "free(copy)"
          ],
          "error": [
            "alloc(first)",
            "alloc(second)",
            "errdefer free",
            "return error"
          ],
          "moments": [
            {
              "step": 0,
              "note": "Both ways out start with a copy the function owns: the normal path copies the text, the error path copies the first half."
            },
            {
              "step": 1,
              "note": "The normal path writes its defer before anything else can fail, so the free is registered while the copy is still alive."
            },
            {
              "step": 1,
              "note": "The error path asks for its second copy at this same column, and the allocator refuses it."
            },
            {
              "step": 2,
              "note": "The normal path reads the copy; the error path gives the first copy back now, because errdefer only runs when the scope is failing."
            },
            {
              "step": 3,
              "note": "The normal path settles on its number, and the error path settles on its error. Neither lane has freed its copy on the normal way out yet."
            },
            {
              "step": 4,
              "note": "The deferred free runs last, after the answer is decided, so the returned number is not held in memory the function already gave back."
            },
            {
              "step": 4,
              "note": "Both lanes now hold nothing: one returns a number and a freed copy, the other returns an error with no allocation left behind."
            }
          ]
        },
        "caption": "A defer runs however the scope ends. An errdefer runs only when it ends in error, which is why it frees the first copy on the failing lane and leaves the normal lane to its defer."
      },
      "hints": [
        "Write down what trimmedLength(\"  42  \") has to return before you open the file, and what has to happen to the copy before the function returns.",
        "The copy is made with the allocator the caller handed over, and the defer that frees it goes on the line below.",
        "The length is the length of the trimmed copy, and the trimming is done with the standard library.",
        "Run zig build test and read the failing test name. It names the length the function has to report."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Freeing the copy at the end of the function and nowhere else. A function that returns early, or fails, would skip the free and leak.",
        "Writing the defer before the allocation. The defer names what is being given back, so it has to come after the thing it frees.",
        "Using errdefer for memory that is given back on every path. A defer that runs on the error path only leaves the copy alive when the function succeeds."
      ],
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "Zig language reference: defer",
          "url": "https://ziglang.org/documentation/0.17.0/#defer"
        },
        {
          "label": "Zig language reference: errdefer",
          "url": "https://ziglang.org/documentation/0.17.0/#errdefer"
        }
      ]
    },
